# Exact Equilibrium from Motion That Never Stops

A periodic quantum walk may revisit its initial state forever, so its snapshots need not converge. Its accumulated record behaves differently: every complete collection of periods has exactly the same mean as one period.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The first laboratory makes the central phenomenon visible. Change the number of complete blocks; the empirical bars remain fixed. Run the corresponding numerical calculation below.

{{demo:0}}

## From periodic sums to empirical equilibrium

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the complete-block proof</summary>
Partition the first $qk$ observations into $q$ blocks of length $k$. Periodicity makes every block identical to the first, so the total sum is $q$ times the one-period sum. Dividing by $qk$ cancels $q$, leaving the one-period mean.
</details>

{{visualization:0}}

The instantaneous plot emphasizes why ordinary mixing fails: the state continues moving and recurring even while its temporal average is settled. For background, see the [overview of quantum walks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_walk) and the [definition of Cesàro summation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation).

## Uniformity is a finite criterion

Periodicity guarantees a stable mean, not a uniform one. Compute one period and compare its coordinates with $1/|G|$.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the equivalence argument</summary>
If every complete block is uniform, the case of one block is uniform. Conversely, if the one-period mean is uniform, exact complete-block averaging says every positive number of periods has that same mean. Thus an infinite family of conditions is equivalent to one finite check.
</details>

{{visualization:2}}

## What happens between complete periods?

Write $N=qk+r$ with $0\le r<k$. The $q$ complete blocks contribute exactly; only the prefix of length $r$ can bias the answer.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the arbitrary-window decomposition</summary>
For a periodic vector sequence $p_n$,
$$
\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}p_n=q\sum_{n=0}^{k-1}p_n+\sum_{n=0}^{r-1}p_n.
$$
This identity both accelerates computation and isolates the remainder as the sole source of finite-window error.
</details>

## Where to go next

Spectral degeneracy determines whether the stable mean is uniform, while weak decoherence may turn averaged equilibrium into genuine instantaneous convergence. The [Cayley graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cayley_graph) viewpoint connects these questions to group symmetry, and [spectral graph theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_graph_theory) provides the language for future quantitative bounds.

